<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

  <handout>
        <title>Proving and Disproving Equivalence Relations</title>

        <p>
            Let's prove congruence is an equivalence relation.
        </p>

        <theorem>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>\sim</m> be the relation on <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> defined
                    by <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if <m>a\equiv b \pmod{3}</m>.
                    Then <m>\sim</m> is an equivalence relation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <proof>
            <p>
                For all <m>a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</m> say <m>a\sim b</m> if and only
                if <m>a\equiv b \pmod{3}</m>.
                We will show <m>\sim</m> is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive
                and therefore an equivalence relation.
            </p>

            <p workspace=".5in">
                <term>Reflexive:</term> First we will show <m>\sim</m> is
                reflexive.
                This means we will show
            </p>

            <p>
                Let <m>a \in  <fillin characters="2"/> </m>.
                Note that
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="2"/> \pmod{3}</m>
                because <m> <fillin characters="10"/>.</m> Thus
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \sim  <fillin characters="2"/></m>
                and thus <m>\sim</m> is reflexive.
            </p>

            <p workspace=".5in">
                <term>Symmetric:</term> Now we will show <m>\sim</m> is
                symmetric.
                This means we will show
            </p>

            <p workspace=".5in">
                Suppose <m>a,b  \in  <fillin characters="2"/></m> and assume
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="2"/> \pmod{3}</m>.
                We will show <m> <fillin characters="5"/> </m> or that
                <m> <fillin characters="1"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="1"/> \pmod{3}</m>.
                Since
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="2"/> \pmod{3}</m>
                we know
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/>  \mid <fillin characters="5"/> </m>
                and so there exists <m><fillin characters="20"/>.</m> By
                algebra, we see
            </p>

            <p>
                Since <m> <fillin characters="2"/>\in \mathbb{Z}</m>, we know
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \mid <fillin characters="2"/> </m>
                and
                <m> <fillin characters="5"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="5"/> \pmod{3}</m>,
                as desired.
                Thus, for all <m>a,b\in <fillin characters="2"/></m>, if
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \sim  <fillin characters="5"/></m>
                then
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \sim  <fillin characters="2"/></m>
                and thus <m>\sim</m> is symmetric.
            </p>

            <p workspace=".5in">
                <term>Transitive:</term> Lastly, we will show <m>\sim</m> is
                transitive.
                Suppose <m>a,b,c\in  <fillin characters="2"/></m> such that
                <m>a\sim b</m> and <m> b\sim c</m>.
                Then
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="2"/> \pmod{3}</m>
                and
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="2"/> \pmod{3}</m>.
                We will show <m> <fillin characters="5"/> </m> or that
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="2"/> \pmod{3}</m>.
                Since
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="2"/> \pmod{3}</m>
                we know
            </p>

            <p workspace=".5in">
                Since
                <m> <fillin characters="5"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="5"/> \pmod{3}</m>
                we know
            </p>

            <p workspace="1in">
                By algebra and substitution,
            </p>

            <p>
                Since <m><fillin characters="2"/> \in \mathbb{Z}</m>, the above
                shows that <m> <fillin characters="5"/> </m> and so
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \equiv  <fillin characters="2"/> \pmod{3}</m>.
                Thus, for all, <m>a,b,c\in <fillin characters="2"/></m> if
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \sim  <fillin characters="2"/></m>
                and
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \sim  <fillin characters="2"/></m>
                then
                <m> <fillin characters="2"/> \sim  <fillin characters="2"/></m>
                and thus <m>\sim</m> is transitive.
            </p>
        </proof>

        <p workspace="1in">
            Follow up question: How does this <m>\sim</m> <q>partition</q>
            <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>?
        </p>

        <activity workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Prove or disprove if each relation is reflexive, symmetric,
                    or transitive.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Define the relation <m>\sim</m> on <m>\mathbb{R}</m> as
                        follows: for all <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}</m>,
                        <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if <m>ab\geq 0</m>.
                        Then <m>\sim</m> is reflexive, symmetric, and
                        transitive.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>U</m> be some nonempty universal set and define
                        the relation <m>\sim</m> on <m>\mathcal{P}(U)</m> as
                        follows: for all <m>A,B\in \mathcal{P}(U)</m>,
                        <m>A\sim B</m> if and only if
                        <m>A\cap B = \emptyset</m>.
                        Then <m>\sim</m> is reflexive, symmetric, and
                        transitive.
                        <em>Hint: You can try this with  <m>U=\{1,2,3\}</m> as an example warmup.</em>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <em>Challenge Question/Proof</em> Define the relation on
                        <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> as follows: for all
                        <m>a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</m>, <m>a\sim b</m> if and only if
                        <m>2a + 3b\equiv 0\pmod{5}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </handout>